Hello Laura,

an editorial lead on a point that tends to be glossed over in this software
category: where a dimension comes from.

Solidon3D reads downloaded meshes and makes their features editable without
CAD. With version 0.5.0 two things changed that are relevant for a
manufacturing readership.

First, the result is an exact body. Primitives are built with real faces and
edges, and holes, slots, countersinks, bosses, beads, grooves and threads stay
exact when they are moved, duplicated, rotated or removed. STEP goes in and
comes back out cleanly. Two geometry kernels run under one interface, and the
options that used to expose that choice have been removed — the software keeps
the exact body where it can and names the limit where it cannot, rather than
converting silently.

Second, and this is the part I would offer as the angle: every dimension
states its provenance. The feature window says whether a value was measured on
the mesh, fitted to a surface, or taken from the construction step that
created it. For anyone documenting a part, that distinction is the difference
between a number and a specification. In the same spirit, a recognised feature
keeps its identity through edits: closing a hole and drilling a new one
produces identical geometry but a different feature, and any fit pointing at
it loses its reference — moving it does not.

Other items: a honeycomb pattern, knurl or rib structure is recognised as one
feature with pitch, cell width and depth; an imported thread is measured
(pitch, starts, handedness, nominal diameter) and can have its counterpart
generated in a table size as a fit; resin printers are supported with pixel
size and minimum wall; fits are checked on the real bodies in their assembled
position.

70 user-visible changes in this release; 896 since the first 0.1 demo.

